WEBVTT

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:19.573
Bring a JasperReports project into Open Reports from its editable JRXML source files. Folder import can convert related reports together, but conversion is a reviewable starting point rather than a promise of exact Jasper parity.

00:00:25.941 --> 00:00:33.822
Choose the small fictional receipt project. It contains a parent report and a reusable header report, both as JRXML source.

00:00:36.455 --> 00:00:45.379
Review the conversion rows before saving. Check which files converted, which need attention, and whether the parent’s subreport source matched the header file.

00:00:49.976 --> 00:01:02.634
Save the converted files as editable report drafts. The importer links a child JRXML when it can identify one unambiguously; the result is Open Reports JSON, not a compiled .jasper file.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:21.037
Open the parent receipt and inspect its migration notes. This example has a static heading and a linked child header; the review panel identifies source features that still need your attention.

00:01:23.709 --> 00:01:34.999
JRXML SQL is not executed during import, and Java or Groovy expressions are not run. Reconnect data in your host application and replace or rewrite unsupported expressions safely.

00:01:39.712 --> 00:01:51.234
Open Code to inspect the converted JSON definition. It is now the editable report format; compare its sections, page size, and child report reference with the original JRXML.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:02:09.137
Preview the converted receipt to confirm the parent and header render together. Then check spacing and pagination against a Jasper-generated PDF using the same data before migrating production output.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:21.101
Import the whole folder again when related sources change, and keep the original JRXML as your migration reference until the output has been validated.
